# Transport and Signaling
#
# Proteins sorted into membranes and vesicles, SNARE fusion in its single home, receptors that switch states, and the apoptosis program, written as state machines.

#>cell_biology_signaling
#:layer #!reference
#:domain #!cell_biology
#:form #!state_machine
#:level #!advanced
#:review #!adversarial_checked
#:valence #!bone

^"emit"{
 ~#title: "Transport and Signaling"
 ~#claim: "Vesicles bud, tether, and fuse through coat proteins, Rab switches, and zippering SNAREs, while receptors and caspases move between discrete states that a cell reads as signals."
 ~#proof: "Signal hypothesis of Blobel, sec genes of Schekman, the SNARE hypothesis of Sollner and Rothman, G proteins of Gilman, the receptor structure of Kobilka, and the apoptosome of Wang and Akey."
 ~#door: "Walk the vesicle machine first, then the receptor machine, then the apoptosis machine."
 ~#audience: "advanced students who know what a protein fold is"
 continuity: "Transport and Signaling | zippering SNAREs"
 cold_open: "Vesicles bud, tether, and fuse when zippering SNAREs pull two membranes together, and receptors switch states a cell can read."
}

